Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Weak/intermittent urinary stream, Nocturia/urinary frequency, Symmetrically enlarged smooth prostate, Increased post-void residual

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-007
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe lower urinary tract symptoms, causing profound sleep deprivation and threatening acute urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, worsening urinary hesitancy with prolonged straining and multiple episodes of near-retention over the past 24 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was strictly necessary to measure bladder volume, rule out acute complete obstruction, and initiate alpha-blocker therapy to prevent a highly probable emergency admission before the next available clinic appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am up ten times a night straining to pass just a few drops, and I feel like my bladder is never empty."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing insidiously over months but acutely worsened over the last 3 days. The patient reports severe progressive hesitancy with delayed initiation of micturition. Their urinary stream is weak, intermittent, and frequently completely interrupted. They experience a constant sensation of incomplete bladder emptying immediately following voiding. Significant straining and the use of abdominal musculature are required to pass urine, followed by terminal dribbling. Nocturia has increased to 8-10 times per night, leading to profound sleep disruption and exhaustion. They also describe sudden, compelling urinary urgency occasionally resulting in urge incontinence.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely fatigued due to lack of sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of nocturnal falls due to frequent, urgent rushing to the bathroom in the dark while exhausted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.6C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 92 Kg
General: Visibly exhausted, frustrated, and anxious about their urinary function.
Physical Exam: Abdomen: Suprapubic area is full but not exquisitely tender; palpable bladder edge noted. Genitourinary: Digital rectal examination reveals a symmetrically enlarged smooth prostate, firm in consistency, with an obliterated median sulcus. No discrete nodules or induration felt. Bedside ultrasound demonstrates an increased post-void residual volume of 250mL. Expected formal uroflowmetry would show a weak/intermittent urinary stream with a maximum flow rate less than 15 mL/s.
Environmental Observations: Rugs present between the bedroom and bathroom posing a significant trip hazard during nocturnal voiding.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently taking over-the-counter cold and flu medication containing pseudoephedrine, which has acutely exacerbated the prostatic outflow resistance.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Prostate Cancer - Ruled out/unlikely due to the smooth, symmetrical, and non-nodular feeling of the prostate on digital rectal examination.

Acute Prostatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, pelvic pain, and lack of tenderness on prostate examination.

Complete Acute Urinary Retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to the ability to still pass small volumes and a post-void residual that, while elevated, is not massively distended (>800mL) or causing agonizing pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing an acute, severe exacerbation of chronic benign prostatic hyperplasia, precipitated by the use of an over-the-counter alpha-agonist (pseudoephedrine). The presentation borders on acute urinary retention, with an elevated post-void residual and severe outflow straining. Delaying clinical assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of complete bladder obstruction requiring emergency catheterization, as well as an ongoing severe fall risk due to sleep-deprived nocturnal bathroom visits. Immediate physical evaluation, cessation of the precipitating medication, and initiation of targeted medical therapy were mandatory under MBS urgent care criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate cessation of the pseudoephedrine-containing cold medication. Commenced oral Tamsulosin 400 micrograms daily, to be taken after dinner. Ordered routine bloods including UECs and PSA for the upcoming GP follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce nighttime bladder filling, the patient is instructed to establish a strict fluid restriction protocol after 6:00 PM. Experiencing fewer awakenings and a more rested morning will act as powerful positive reinforcement to maintain this evening fluid curtailment habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urinal bottle provided for the bedside to eliminate the need to walk to the bathroom during the night, reducing fall risk.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours to assess the efficacy of Tamsulosin and review formal blood results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required if the patient becomes completely unable to pass any urine for more than 6 hours, or develops severe, agonizing lower abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Tamsulosin can occasionally cause dizziness when standing up rapidly; caution is advised.
Clinical Course: The urinary stream should improve, and the feeling of incomplete emptying should lessen within 48 to 72 hours of stopping the cold medication and starting the new prescription.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you stop passing urine entirely, or if your lower stomach becomes extremely painful and hard, you must go to the Emergency Department immediately for a catheter.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The cold medication you took caused the muscles around your prostate to clamp down tightly, restricting your urine flow. Stopping that medication and starting the new tablet will relax those muscles, allowing the bladder to empty more effectively.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking the cold medication immediately. Do not drink any fluids after 6:00 PM. Use the bedside urinal to prevent falling in the dark.

